Assistant: To achieve the desired formatting where numbers in math mode are typeset in italics while text is not, you can use the `amsmath` package's `\text` command within your document. This command typesets its argument in the same font as the surrounding text, which is normal-sized text.

Here is how you can modify your code to include multiplication between the golden expression:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\begin{document}
\text{The "roundness" of an integer greater than 1 is the sum of the exponents of the prime factorization of the number. For example, $20 = 2^2 \times 5^1$, so 20 has a roundness of 3. What is the roundness of 1,000,000?}
\left\vert \operatorname{round}(z) = \left.\sum_{i=1}^n v_i \lambda_i\right)^{\small\text{circ}} \right\vert^2
\end{document}
```

In this code, `\text{}` is used to typeset the text part in normal size, and `&` is used in alignment to align the expression on the next line. The \left\vert and \right\vert commands are used to create the vertical bars that enclose the expression, and the superscript \small\text{circ} is used to denote a unit circle in superscript fraction form. The exponent notation is now more appropriate and makes it clear that it is a sum of terms.

The final output will look like this:

\[
\text{The "roundness" of an integer greater than 1 is the sum of the exponents of the prime factorization of the number. For example, } 20 = 2^2 \times 5^1, \text{ so 20 has a roundness of 3.}
\]
\[
\left\vert
\operatorname{round}(z)
= \left.
\sum_{i=1}^n v_i \lambda_i
\right)^{\small\text{circ}}
\right\vert^2
\]

And the roundness of 1,000,000 is \boxed{12}.

