Since $AB = AD = 2r$ and $BD = 2 + 2r$, we have $2(2r)^2 = (2 + 2r)^2$. So \[
1+2r+r^{2}=2r^{2}, \quad \text{and} \quad r^{2}-2r-1=0.
\]Applying the quadratic formula gives $r=\boxed{1+\sqrt{2}}$.

We first compute the area of $ABCD.$ A quick way to do so (besides the shoelace formula) is to draw the rectangle with vertices $A=(0,0),$ $(0,3),$ $(4,3),$ and $(4,0),$ and divide the part of the rectangle outside $ABCD$ into squares and right triangles, as shown:[asy]
size(5cm);
draw((-1,0)--(5,0),EndArrow);
draw((0,-1)--(0,4),EndArrow);
label("$x$",(5,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,4),N);
for (int i=1; i<=4; ++i)
{
	draw((i,-.15)--(i,.15));
    if (i < 4)
    	draw((-.15,i)--(.15,i));
}
pair A=(0,0), B=(1,2), C=(3,3), D=(4,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--A);
draw((0,0)--(0,3)--(4,3)--(4,0)--cycle,dashed);
draw((0,2)--(1,2)--(1,3),dashed);
dot("$A$",A,SW);
dot("$B$",B,NW);
dot("$C$",C,N);
dot("$D$",D,S);
[/asy]Then \[[ABCD] = 12 - 2 \cdot 1 - 1 - \tfrac32 = \tfrac{15}2.\]Therefore, the two pieces of $ABCD$ must each have area $\tfrac12 \cdot \tfrac{15}2 = \tfrac{15}4.$

Let $E$ be the point where the line through $A$ intersects $\overline{CD},$ as shown:
[asy]
size(5cm);
draw((-1,0)--(5,0),EndArrow);
draw((0,-1)--(0,4),EndArrow);
label("$x$",(5,0),E);
